[{'["# Feature name: Vertical_Hydro_Road_Interaction\\n# Feature description: Calculates the product of Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways. This interaction term captures the combined effect of terrain elevation relative to water and proximity to roads.\\n# Usefulness: This feature captures ecological gradients influenced by water sources and human infrastructure. Areas closer to roads and with particular vertical water distances might correlate with forest types affected by accessibility and water availability.\\n\\ndf[\'Vertical_Hydro_Road_Interaction\'] = df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] * df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\']", "# Feature name: Hillshade_Range\\n# Feature description: Calculates the range (max - min) of hillshade values at 9am, noon, and 3pm.\\n# Usefulness: A large range in hillshade values indicates more variation in sunlight exposure on the terrain, which can significantly impact the types of vegetation and forest cover.\\n\\ndf[\'Hillshade_Range\'] = df[[\'Hillshade_9am\', \'Hillshade_Noon\', \'Hillshade_3pm\']].max(axis=1) - df[[\'Hillshade_9am\', \'Hillshade_Noon\', \'Hillshade_3pm\']].min(axis=1)", "# Feature name: Hydrology_Effective_Elevation\\n# Feature description: Calculates the difference between Elevation and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology, representing the effective elevation above the nearest water source.\\n# Usefulness: Effective elevation relative to hydrology significantly impacts vegetation due to moisture availability, especially at different altitudes.\\n\\ndf[\'Hydrology_Effective_Elevation\'] = df[\'Elevation\'] - df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\']", "# Feature name: Fire_Road_Hillshade_Comb\\n# Feature description: Calculates a combined feature: (Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points / Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways) * Hillshade_Mean.\\n# Usefulness: Combines the effects of fire proximity, road proximity, and sunlight exposure in a single feature, which could highlight areas with unique ecological conditions.\\n\\ndf[\'Fire_Road_Hillshade_Comb\'] = (df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\'] / (df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\'] + 0.01)) * df[\'Hillshade_Mean\']", "# Feature name: Soil_Type_Interaction\\n# Feature description: Combines Soil_Type_Count with the Elevation feature by multiplying both.\\n# Usefulness: By interacting soil richness with elevation, this feature captures the interplay between soil diversity and elevation, which affects forest growth and type.\\n\\ndf[\'Soil_Type_Interaction\'] = df[\'Soil_Type_Count\'] * df[\'Elevation\']", "# Feature name: Aspect_cosine\\n# Feature description: Applies the cosine transformation to the Aspect feature to complement the Aspect_sine feature.\\n# Usefulness: While Aspect_sine captures east-west orientation, Aspect_cosine captures north-south directional effects, providing a more holistic understanding of the terrain\'s aspect.\\n\\ndf[\'Aspect_cosine\'] = np.cos(np.radians(df[\'Aspect\']))", "# Feature name: Road_Fire_Hydrology_Sum\\n# Feature description: Calculates the sum of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\\n# Usefulness: Aggregating these three distance measures captures the overall accessibility and proximity to key landscape features, which heavily influence forest growth.\\n\\ndf[\'Road_Fire_Hydrology_Sum\'] = df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] + df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\'] + df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\']", "# Feature name: Area_Type_Combination\\n# Feature description: Combines wilderness area indicators into a single feature by one-hot encoding the categorical wilderness areas.\\n# Usefulness: Consolidating wilderness area indicators into a single categorical variable clarifies the analysis and allows the model to learn on the basis of unique wilderness areas.\\n\\n# One-hot encode the wilderness areas\\ndf[\'Wilderness_Area_comb\'] = df[[\'Wilderness_Area1\', \'Wilderness_Area2\', \'Wilderness_Area3\', \'Wilderness_Area4\']].idxmax(axis=1)"]': 0.007446657854984884}, {'["# Feature name: Elevation_zscore\\n# Feature description: Z-score normalization of the Elevation feature, which scales the data relative to its mean and standard deviation. This is helpful for comparing Elevation relative to the dataset distribution.\\n# Usefulness: Elevation is strongly correlated with forest cover type, so normalizing it allows models to consider it effectively alongside other features that may have different scaling.\\n\\ndf[\'Elevation_zscore\'] = (df[\'Elevation\'] - 3024.5820) / 201.5675", "# Feature name: Distance_To_Hydrology_Magnitude\\n# Feature description: Calculates the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using both horizontal and vertical distances.\\n# Usefulness: Combining horizontal and vertical distances to hydrology provides a better understanding of proximity to water sources in 3D space, which can significantly influence forest cover type.\\n\\ndf[\'Distance_To_Hydrology_Magnitude\'] = np.sqrt(df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\']2 + df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\']2)", "# Feature name: Hillshade_Mean\\n# Feature description: Mean value of hillshade indices (9am, noon, 3pm) to summarize overall sunlight exposure for the terrain.\\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure is a critical factor for vegetation growth, and combining the three hillshade values into an average can simplify analysis while retaining meaningful information.\\n\\ndf[\'Hillshade_Mean\'] = df[[\'Hillshade_9am\', \'Hillshade_Noon\', \'Hillshade_3pm\']].mean(axis=1)", "# Feature name: Aspect_sine\\n# Feature description: Applies the sine function to the Aspect feature to transform it into angular data and highlight directional information.\\n# Usefulness: The sine transformation helps capture directional effects (e.g., north-facing vs. south-facing slopes), which can influence temperature and moisture retention and thus vegetation or fores